| l.Json           |                |                  |                 |\n","exit":0,"description":"Search BDD test packages","truncated":false},"title":"Search BDD test packages","time":{"start":1782088282192,"end":1782088282202}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Good. NuGet works and the ModelContextProtocol package is available (v1.4.0). I'll use the official Microsoft C# MCP SDK to expose an MCP server with stdio transport, exposing tools that map to query functions.\n\nPlan:\n- Solution: BrazilianSoccerMcp.sln\n- Projects:\n  - BrazilianSoccerMcp.Server (console app, MCP server over stdio with tools)\n  - BrazilianSoccerMcp.Core (library with data loaders, models, query services, team normalization)\n  - BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests (xUnit, BDD-style tests with Gherkin-ish naming using Xunit theories + descriptive names)\n\nLet me create the project structure. I'll create everything with file writes.\n\nLet me think about the design:\n\nModels:\n- MatchRecord (the unified representation across all match CSVs)\n  - Competition: enum (Brasileirao, CopaDoBrasil, Libertadores, BR_Football_Extended, HistoricalBrasileirao) - but BR-Football has many tournaments\n  - Date: DateTime\n  - HomeTeam: string (normalized name)\n  - AwayTeam: string (normalized name)\n  - HomeGoal: int\n  - AwayGoal: int\n  - Season: int\n  - Round: string (optional)\n  - Stage: string (optional, Libertadores)\n  - Arena: string (optional, historical)\n  - HomeState: string (optional)\n  - AwayState: string (optional)\n  - HasStats: bool (corner, attack, shots - from BR-Football)\n  - HomeCorner, AwayCorner, HomeAttack, AwayAttack, HomeShots, AwayShots: double?\n\n- Player (FIFA player data)\n  - ID, Name, Age, Nationality, Overall, Potential, Club, Position, JerseyNumber, Height, Weight, PreferredFoot\n  - plus skill ratings\n\nQuery Service - expose methods like:\n- FindMatchesByTeam(teamName, competition?, season?, dateRange?)\n- FindMatchesBetweenTeams(teamA, teamB, competition?, season?)\n- GetTeamStats(teamName, season?, competition?)\n- GetHeadToHead(teamA, teamB)\n- FindPlayers(name?, nationality?, club?, position?, minOverall?)\n- GetTopPlayers(...)\n- GetStandings(season, competition)\n- GetBiggestWins(...)\n- AverageGoalsPerMatch(competition?, season?)\n- FindMostRecentMatch(teamA, teamB)\n\nTeam Name Normalization:\n- Strip \"-SP\" state suffix\n- Strip \" - RJ\" state suffix (with space dash)\n- Handle parentheses like \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" -> hmm tricky; the core name with parenthetical might be kept; just strip state suffix\n- Handle accents: keep as is (UTF-8). Provide normalization for comparison? Should probably compare case-insensitively, ignoring accents too. Let's create a normalized key that strips state suffix, trims, removes accent marks, lowercases.\n\nDate parsing:\n- ISO: \"2023-09-24\"\n- Brazilian: \"29/03/2003\"\n- With time: \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\"\n\nFor CSV parsing, I'll use the CsvHelper package or just a simple hand-rolled parser. Let me check CsvHelper.\n\nActually given varied CSV formats (some quoted, some not, headers in different styles), I'll use CsvHelper. Let me check if it's installable. Actually, let me hand-roll a simple CSV reader to avoid dependency issues. But CsvHelper is well known. Let me try.\n\nActually, I'll write a simple CSV parser since the formats are not too complex (quoting only when needed). Let me make it robust enough to handle quoted fields with commas.\n\nLet me now design the MCP server tools. With MCP C# SDK 1.4.0, the pattern is:\n- Use [McpServerToolType] class with [McpServerTool] methods, or use McpServerTool.CreateDelegate.\n- A standard stdio MCP server with `McpServerServer` and stdio transport.\n\nLet me look at the SDK API. I'll use the stdio approach:\n\n```csharp\nvar builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);\nbuilder.Logging.AddConsole(o => o.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Warning); // avoid stdout\nbuilder.Services.AddMcpServer()\n    .WithStdioServerTransport()\n    .WithToolsFromAssembly();\nawait builder.Build().RunAsync();\n```\n\nAnd tools:\n```csharp\n[McpServerToolType]\npublic static class SoccerTools\n{\n    [McpServerTool, Description(\"...\")]\n    public static string FindMatchesBetweenTeams(...) { ... }\n}\n```\n\nThe methods can take parameters and return strings (which get serialized).\n\nI'll structure the tools to wrap the query service.\n\nLet me also create BDD tests. The BDD style with xUnit can be done with test names like \"FindMatchesBetweenTeams_GivenDataLoaded_WhenSearchFlamengoFluminense_ThenReturnsMatches\". Or use Xunit theories with InlineData. Or use a BDD framework like LightBDD or SpecFlow. SpecFlow requires additional setup. I'll go with LightBDD.XUnit2 if available, or write tests in Given/When/Then style with xUnit using descriptive test names and arrange/act/assert comments.\n\nLet me check LightBDD.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_eecbd2ab6001lNFbPxypgwHkns","sessionID":"ses_11343aad7ffeUJRsKWvvH5xMok","messageID":"msg_eecbcee2e00164N9qQ6pI3JmV3"}}
