text":"Let me check the LightBDD.XUnit21984 API. I recall LightBDD uses [FeatureDescriptor] / [Scenario] and `Runner.WithBdd` patterns. LightBDD.XUnit2 v3.x uses `[Scenario]` attribute on test methods within a class, with `IBddRunner` via `Composite.New`. Actually the modern LightBDD API (v3) is:\n\n```csharp\n[Feature]\npublic class MyFeature\n{\n    [Scenario]\n    public void My_scenario()\n    {\n        Runner.Run(b => b\n            .AddAsyncStep(\"Given ...\", step1)\n            ...\n        );\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, the API changed across versions. Let me check the installed package's docs/examples to use the right API. Let me look at the LightBDD framework xml or a quick search. Actually I recall the LightBDD.XUnit2 v3 API:\n\n```csharp\nusing LightBDD.XUnit2;\nusing LightBDD.Framework;\nusing LightBDD.Framework.Scenarios;\n\n[FeatureDescription(\"...\")]\npublic class MyClass\n{\n    [Scenario]\n    public void Scenario_name()\n    {\n        Composite.New()\n            .AddSteps(...)\n            .Run();\n    }\n}\n```\n\nActually the simplest and most reliable LightBDD v3 API uses `Runner.Run(...)` from a `IBddRunner` created via `new XUnitBddRunner(this)`. Hmm.\n\nLet me check the actual API by inspecting the package assemblies. Let me find the xml docs.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_eecc6638f001smoeCcCm7wql1A","sessionID":"ses_11343aad7ffeUJRsKWvvH5xMok","messageID":"msg_eecc64d0c001jfu1NSx18eBME6"}}
