ith default test output.\r\n            </summary>\r\n        </member>\r\n--\n            Constructor initializing <see cref=\"P:LightBDD.XUnit2.FeatureFixture.Runner\"/> for feature class instance as well as configures <see cref=\"P:LightBDD.XUnit2.FeatureFixture.TestOutput\"/> with <paramref name=\"output\"/>.\r\n            </summary>\r\n        </member>\r\n---NoContext---\n            <see cref=\"T:LightBDD.Framework.IBddRunner`1\"/> extensions.\r\n            Method allowing to retrieve the <see cref=\"T:LightBDD.Framework.IBddRunner\"/> instance from <see cref=\"T:LightBDD.Core.Extensibility.IFeatureRunner\"/>.\r\n            The <see cref=\"T:LightBDD.Framework.IBddRunner\"/> is a main runner interface that should be used for executing LightBDD scenarios, while the interfaces coming from LightBDD.Core namespace should be used only in integration projects.\r\n        <member name=\"T:LightBDD.Framework.IBddRunner`1\">\r\n            See also: <seealso cref=\"T:LightBDD.Framework.IBddRunner\"/>.\r\n        <member name=\"T:LightBDD.Framework.IBddRunner\">\r\n        <member name=\"T:LightBDD.Framework.NoContext\">\r\n            Extensions allowing to create contextual <see cref=\"T:LightBDD.Framework.IBddRunner\"/> instance.\r\n            <param name=\"runner\"><see cref=\"T:LightBDD.Framework.IBddRunner\"/> instance.</param>\r\n            <param name=\"runner\"><see cref=\"T:LightBDD.Framework.IBddRunner\"/> instance.</param>\r\n            <param name=\"runner\"><see cref=\"T:LightBDD.Framework.IBddRunner\"/> instance.</param>\r\n            <param name=\"runner\"><see cref=\"T:LightBDD.Framework.IBddRunner\"/> instance.</param>\r\n            <param name=\"runner\"><see cref=\"T:LightBDD.Framework.IBddRunner\"/> instance.</param>\r\n","exit":0,"description":"Find Runner property and IBddRunner members","truncated":false},"title":"Find Runner property and IBddRunner members","time":{"start":1782088962167,"end":1782088962169}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The `ExtendedExtensions.RunScenario<T>(IBddRunner<T>, params Expression<Action<T>>[])` takes expression trees — the step names come from the expression body (method names or text). This is the modern fluent API: you write `Runner.RunScenario(_ => Given_data_loaded(), _ => When_search(\"Flam\",\"Flu\"), ...)` and LightBDD formats step text from the lambda body including arguments.\n\nActually the \"Extended\" API uses `Expression<Action<T>>` and the step name is derived from the expression — e.g. `_ => When_I_search_for_matches_between(\"Flamengo\",\"Fluminense\")` would produce step text \"When I search for matches between \\\"Flamengo\\\",\\\"Fluminense\\\"\" (LightBDD splits Pascal case into words and includes args).\n\nThe \"Basic\" `RunScenario(IBddRunner, params Action[])` — step names come from delegate method names. Method group `Given_the_match_data_is_loaded` → \"Given the match data is loaded\" (Pascal → words).\n\nFor the BDD style matching the spec's Gherkin (Given/When/Then), I'll use the Basic API with method groups, OR Extended with lambdas. The Extended API gives nicer step text with parameters. But `IBddRunner<T>` is generic — `T` is the context type. The non-generic `IBddRunner` (LightBDD.Framework.IBddRunner) is `NoContext`. `FeatureFixture.Runner` returns `IBddRunner` (non-generic = IBddRunner<NoContext>?). Let me check: `FeatureFixture.Runner` property type. The CompactExtensions.AddStep takes IScenarioBuilder<T> — so I need to call `Runner.NewScenario()`? Or `Runner.Run(...)`.\n\nHmm. Let me look for how to start a scenario. The `IBddRunner` likely has `NewScenario()` returning IScenarioBuilder<NoContext>. Let me check.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_eecc781660012Z1t73y2hW9r2Q","sessionID":"ses_11343aad7ffeUJRsKWvvH5xMok","messageID":"msg_eecc76038001CX3kiDD3b42bQE"}}
