{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1782088983034,"sessionID":"ses_11343aad7ffeUJRsKWvvH5xMok","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_20f5049f3fb14fdca76181ad","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -A8 'T:LightBDD.XUnit2.LightBddScopeAttribute' /Users/maui/.nuget/packages/lightbdd.xunit2/3.12.0/lib/net6.0/LightBDD.XUnit2.xml | head -20; echo \"---FeatureFixtureAttribute usage---\"; grep -A6 'T:LightBDD.XUnit2.FeatureFixtureAttribute' /Users/maui/.nuget/packages/lightbdd.xunit2/3.12.0/lib/net6.0/LightBDD.XUnit2.xml | head -15","description":"Check LightBddScope and FeatureFixture attrs"},"output":"        <member name=\"T:LightBDD.XUnit2.LightBddScopeAttribute\">\r\n            <summary>\r\n            An attribute allowing to initialize and finalize LightBDD in XUnit framework.\r\n            \r\n            The <c>[assembly:LightBddScope]</c> attribute has to be present in assembly containing LightBDD tests.\r\n            It is possible to customize the LightBDD configuration by overriding the <see cref=\"M:LightBDD.XUnit2.LightBddScopeAttribute.OnConfigure(LightBDD.Core.Configuration.LightBddConfiguration)\"/>() method,\r\n            as well as execute code before any test and after all tests by overriding the <see cref=\"M:LightBDD.XUnit2.LightBddScopeAttribute.OnSetUp\"/>() / <see cref=\"M:LightBDD.XUnit2.LightBddScopeAttribute.OnTearDown\"/>() methods.\r\n            </summary>\r\n        </member>\r\n---FeatureFixtureAttribute usage---\n        <member name=\"T:LightBDD.XUnit2.FeatureFixtureAttribute\">\r\n            <summary>\r\n            Attribute that should be applied to all test classes representing feature tests.\r\n            For XUnit integration this attribute is meaningless and it is present for backward compatibility.\r\n            </summary>\r\n        </member>\r\n        <member name=\"T:LightBDD.XUnit2.FeatureRunnerProvider\">\r\n","metadata":{"output":"        <member name=\"T:LightBDD.XUnit2.LightBddScopeAttribute\">\r\n            <summary>\r\n            An attribute allowing to initialize and finalize LightBDD in XUnit framework.\r\n            \r\n            The <c>[assembly:LightBddScope]</c> attribute has to be present in assembly containing LightBDD tests.\r\n            It is possible to customize the LightBDD configuration by overriding the <see cref=\"M:LightBDD.XUnit2.LightBddScopeAttribute.OnConfigure(LightBDD.Core.Configuration.LightBddConfiguration)\"/>() method,\r\n            as well as execute code before any test and after all tests by overriding the <see cref=\"M:LightBDD.XUnit2.LightBddScopeAttribute.OnSetUp\"/>() / <see cref=\"M:LightBDD.XUnit2.LightBddScopeAttribute.OnTearDown\"/>() methods.\r\n            </summary>\r\n        </member>\r\n---FeatureFixtureAttribute usage---\n        <member name=\"T:LightBDD.XUnit2.FeatureFixtureAttribute\">\r\n            <summary>\r\n            Attribute that should be applied to all test classes representing feature tests.\r\n            For XUnit integration this attribute is meaningless and it is present for backward compatibility.\r\n            </summary>\r\n        </member>\r\n        <member name=\"T:LightBDD.XUnit2.FeatureRunnerProvider\">\r\n","exit":0,"description":"Check LightBddScope and FeatureFixture attrs","truncated":false},"title":"Check LightBddScope and FeatureFixture attrs","time":{"start":1782088983030,"end":1782088983032}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"`IBddRunner` is the non-generic which is `IBddRunner<NoContext>`. The `FeatureFixture.Runner` returns `IBddRunner`. The `BasicExtensions.RunScenario(IBddRunner, params Action[])` accepts the non-generic IBddRunner. Good. So I can do:\n\n```csharp\nRunner.RunScenario(\n    Given_data_loaded,\n    When_search_flamengo_fluminense,\n    Then_returns_matches);\n```\n\nwith void parameterless methods. The step names come from method names (Pascal → spaced words). Good.\n\nFor parameterized steps with the Extended API, I'd need `IBddRunner<T>` and Expression trees. The non-generic `IBddRunner` (= IBddRunner<NoContext>) supports ExtendedExtensions.RunScenario<NoContext> via the generic interface. Hmm, but `IBddRunner` non-generic might not be directly IBddRunner<NoContext>. There's likely a cast or the non-generic inherits. Let me just use the Basic API with method groups — it's the most reliable.\n\nBut I want steps with parameters like \"When I search for matches between Flamengo and Fluminense\". With Basic API method groups, the method name IS the step text. So I'd name the method `When_I_search_for_matches_between_Flamengo_and_Fluminense` — verbose but works and reads as BDD.\n\nAlternatively, use the Extended API with lambdas: `Runner.RunScenario(_ => Given_data_loaded(), _ => When_search(\"Flamengo\",\"Fluminense\"), _ => Then_result_is_nonempty())`. The ExtendedExtensions.RunScenario takes Expression<Action<T>> — the step text is generated from the lambda body including method name + args. But it requires `IBddRunner<T>`. Since FeatureFixture.Runner is `IBddRunner` (non-generic), I need to check if it's also IBddRunner<NoContext>. The non-generic IBddRunner likely IS IBddRunner<NoContext> (alias). Let me just try the Basic API — safest.\n\nI'll write tests using BasicExtensions.RunScenario with method groups. Each test class extends FeatureFixture. Methods are named as Given_/When_/Then_ sentences.\n\nActually, to keep parameters, I can have private helper methods that the step methods call. Eg:\n\n```csharp\n[Scenario]\npublic void Find_matches_between_two_teams()\n{\n    Runner.RunScenario(\n        Given_the_match_data_is_loaded,\n        When_I_search_for_matches_between_Flamengo_and_Fluminense,\n        Then_I_should_receive_a_list_of_matches_with_date_scores_and_competition);\n}\n\nvoid Given_the_match_data_is_loaded() { _data = new BrazilianSoccerData(DataDir); _query = _data.Query; }\nvoid When_I_search_for_matches_between_Flamengo_and_Fluminense() { _result = _query.FindMatchesBetweenTeams(\"Flamengo\",\"Fluminense\"); }\nvoid Then_I_should_receive_a_list_of_matches_with_date_scores_and_competition() { Assert.NotEmpty(_result); foreach(var m in _result){ Assert.True(m.Date != DateTime.MinValue); Assert.True(m.HomeGoal>=0);} }\n```\n\nThat works. Let me write tests using this Basic API.\n\nI also need a LightBddScope. LightBDD.XUnit2 requires a `[LightBddScope]` attribute on the assembly (via an assembly-level attribute) to initialize LightBDD. Actually it's `[assembly: LightBddScope]`. Let me check — there's `LightBddScopeAttribute`. It's an assembly-level attribute that hooks into xUnit framework. Let me add it.\n\nActually `LightBddScopeAttribute` is applied at assembly level: `[assembly: LightBddScope]`. Let me verify by checking its usage. It's described as the integration. I'll add to GlobalUsings or an AssemblyInfo file.\n\nLet me write the test files. I'll create:\n- `AssemblyInfo.cs` with `[assembly: LightBddScope]`\n- `SoccerFixture.cs` base class with DataDir, _data, _query, _result fields\n- `MatchQueryTests.cs`\n- `TeamQueryTests.cs`\n- `PlayerQueryTests.cs`\n- `CompetitionQueryTests.cs`\n- `StatisticsTests.cs`\n- `NormalizationTests.cs` (team name variations, date formats, UTF-8)\n\nLet me check `LightBddScopeAttribute` usage — is it assembly-level? Let me check.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_eecc7d97e001bPXBm1trOV8v3n","sessionID":"ses_11343aad7ffeUJRsKWvvH5xMok","messageID":"msg_eecc79150001xZyKsMj01CR5Rs"}}
