{"type":"text","timestamp":1782089302979,"sessionID":"ses_11343aad7ffeUJRsKWvvH5xMok","part":{"id":"prt_eecccb8d9001AfLwKEMIW3j81R","messageID":"msg_eecccb3940017osL4PmpK9EwW5","sessionID":"ses_11343aad7ffeUJRsKWvvH5xMok","type":"text","text":"Done. Implemented a C# / .NET 10 Brazilian Soccer MCP server per `TASK.md`:\n\n- **`BrazilianSoccerMcp.Core`** — domain models, a dependency-free CSV reader, multi-format date parser, team-name normalizer (state-suffix stripping, accent folding, **same-base disambiguation** so `Atletico-MG` ≠ `Atletico-PR`), and a `SoccerQueryService` covering match / team / competition / player / statistical queries.\n- **`BrazilianSoccerMcp.Server`** — stdio MCP server using the official `ModelContextProtocol` 1.4.0 SDK, exposing 16 tools that return the spec's response formats (verified via real JSON-RPC `tools/list` + `tools/call`; 2019 standings correctly show Flamengo as champion with 90 pts).\n- **`BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests`** — 22 BDD (Given/When/Then) scenarios with LightBDD + xUnit across all required categories plus data-quality (name variations, accents, multi-format dates, all 6 CSVs loadable).\n\nFinal status: `dotnet build BrazilianSoccerMcp.slnx` succeeds with 0 warnings/errors; `dotnet test` → **22 passed, 0 failed**. README updated. No git operations performed (not requested).","time":{"start":1782089300185,"end":1782089302976}}}
